We can use the AM-GM inequality to find the minimum value of $(x + y)(y + z)$. First, we note that $xyz(x + y + z) = 1$ implies that $xyz = \frac{1}{x + y + z}$. We can then rewrite the expression as follows: \begin{align*} (x + y)(y + z) &= xy + y^2 + yz + yz + yz + yz \\ &= xy + y^2 + 3yz + yz \\ &= xy + y^2 + 4yz. \end{align*} By AM-GM, we have \begin{align*} xy + y^2 + 4yz &\geq 3\sqrt[3]{xy \cdot y^2 \cdot 4yz} \\ &= 3\sqrt[3]{4x y^3 z} \\ &= 3\sqrt[3]{4 \cdot \frac{1}{x + y + z} \cdot y^3} \\ &= 3\sqrt[3]{\frac{4y^3}{x + y + z}}. \end{align*} To minimize this expression, we need to maximize $x + y + z$. By AM-GM, we have \begin{align*} x + y + z &\geq 3\sqrt[3]{xyz} \\ &= 3\sqrt[3]{\frac{1}{x + y + z}}. \end{align*} Therefore, \begin{align*} x + y + z &\geq 3\sqrt[3]{\frac{1}{x + y + z}} \\ \left(x + y + z\right)^4 &\geq 27 \\ x + y + z &\geq 3. \end{align*} Substituting this back into our expression, we have \begin{align*} (x + y)(y + z) &\geq 3\sqrt[3]{\frac{4y^3}{x + y + z}} \\ &\geq 3\sqrt[3]{\frac{4y^3}{3}} \\ &= 3\sqrt[3]{\frac{4}{3}}y. \end{align*} To minimize this expression, we need to minimize $y$. By AM-GM, we have \begin{align*} y &\geq \sqrt{xz} \\ &\geq \sqrt{\frac{1}{(x + y + z)^2}} \\ &\geq \frac{1}{3}. \end{align*} Therefore, \begin{align*} (x + y)(y + z) &\geq 3\sqrt[3]{\frac{4}{3}} \cdot \frac{1}{3} \\ &= \frac{4}{3}. \end{align*} Equality occurs when $x = y = z = \frac{1}{3}$, so the minimum value of $(x + y)(y + z)$ is $\boxed{\frac{4}{3}}$.The answer is: \frac{4}{3}
